From e231a91577a8d59811baa38c72e3984a3556422d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Schmidt <sebastian.schmidt@lasp.colorado.edu>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 20:43:26 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Local workaround: include already-cached files in
 download_*_https() return value

download_laads_https()/download_lance_https()/download_nsidc_https() in
er3t/util/daac.py silently drop a file from their returned list when it's
already cached on disk and passes final_file_check() -- only exist_count
is incremented, fnames_local never gets the entry. A caller re-running
against previously-downloaded granules (every repeat run of a trajectory-
tracking pipeline against the same date/tile) sees an empty/partial list
and cannot tell a real cache hit from a failed download. Documented
independently in Libera camera/libera-cam's apps/l2/fetch_proxy_scene.py,
which worked around it with its own glob-based fallback wrapper.

Fix: track two lists instead of one. fnames_local now accumulates every
tag-matching file (cached or not) and is what gets returned, as the
docstring already promised. fnames_to_download is new and holds only the
files that still need fetching -- it stays index-aligned with
primary_commands/backup_commands for the run-loop below, which previously
indexed into fnames_local itself and would have gone out of sync (or
associated the wrong command with the wrong file) the moment a cached
file was folded into that same list.

Follow-up (2026-10-24): the three "Total of {} will be downloaded. {}
will be skipped" messages were left reading len(fnames_local) for the
"will be downloaded" count -- which, after the fix above, is the combined
cached+queued total, not just what's queued. A fully-cached granule
(every file already on disk) would print e.g. "Total of 1 will be
downloaded. 1 will be skipped" even though the actual download loop below
(which iterates fnames_to_download/primary_commands, correctly empty in
that case) does nothing -- so nothing was ever actually re-downloaded,
but the message said otherwise and looked like a caching bug from the
caller's side. Now reads len(fnames_to_download) instead, matching what
the loop below actually does.

Follow-up 2 (2026-10-24): both the "File {} already exists..." print and
the "Total of {} will be downloaded..." print in all three functions were
unconditional -- neither ever actually checked `verbose` at all, despite
every caller (including this repo's own satellite.fetch_granule, via its
fetch_verbose parameter) assuming verbose=False would silence them. Found
from real pipeline output showing these messages on every run regardless
of fetch_verbose, which also visibly garbled render.py's new per-item
progress-bar lines (see render.py's _progress() docstring) whenever a
download message printed in between two progress updates. Both prints in
all three functions are now wrapped in `if verbose:`.

Follow-up 3 (2026-10-24): all three download_*_https() functions fall back
to running a backup shell command (wget) via os.system() whenever the
primary one (curl) produces a file that fails final_file_check() -- but
none of them checked that the backup tool is actually installed first. On
a machine without wget (e.g. a stock macOS install, which ships curl but
not wget), the backup attempt failed with an easy-to-miss "sh: wget:
command not found" on stderr, final_file_check() unsurprisingly still
failed afterward, and the download was reported as fully failed -- making
it look like a real second attempt was made and also failed, when the
backup command never actually ran at all. Spotted from real pipeline
output ("[Errno -51] NetCDF: Unknown file format" immediately followed by
"sh: wget: command not found") for two NOAA-20 VIIRS granules that
otherwise downloaded fine moments later via a different candidate
overpass. New helper _backup_download_tool_available() (shutil.which
('wget') is not None) now gates the os.system(backup_commands[i]) call in
all three functions; when it's not installed, a clear one-line message
names the missing tool and suggests `brew install wget` instead of
silently skipping straight to "could not complete the download".

Temporary local fix pending upstream review by Hong -- see
LAGRANGE_ER3T_PATCH.md in the arcsix_lagrange_tracking repo for how to
drop this once er3t's master picks up a real fix.
